EMPLOYER COMPENSATION SIGNAL REGISTRY — CURSOR RULES


WHAT THIS SYSTEM IS

A longitudinal, employer-anchored time-series dataset of compensation ranges derived from public job postings. Not a job board. Not a survey aggregator. Not a general web scraper. A narrow, high-signal registry of target employers with structured collectors that observe those employers systematically over time.

Core differentiation from Levels.fyi: this data reflects what employers advertise they will pay, constrained by pay transparency law and competitive dynamics — not what candidates with strong offers chose to self-report. The moat is longitudinal history. Snapshot history cannot be retroactively acquired.


WHAT THIS SYSTEM IS NOT

- Not a general internet crawler
- Not a GUI agent browsing company websites
- Not trying to achieve full employer coverage on day one
- Not competing with Indeed or LinkedIn on posting volume
- Never mix candidate-reported compensation into the same tables as employer-posted ranges


GEOGRAPHIC SCOPE

Primary metros for v1: Seattle / Washington State broadly, Bay Area, SoCal (LA and San Diego), New York, Boston, Austin, Charlotte. Remote-first employers are their own segment regardless of HQ location.


ROLE FAMILIES

Nine families only. Do not create new ones without explicit instruction.

  swe                   Software Engineering: frontend, backend, fullstack, mobile, generalist
  infra                 Infrastructure: platform eng, SRE, DevOps, cloud eng, systems eng
  data                  Data and ML: data eng, data science, ML eng, AI eng, ML research
  tpm                   Technical Program Management: TPM, EPM, technical project mgmt
  pm                    Product Management: PM, technical PM
  em                    Engineering Management: all levels — uses manager track only
  recruiting            Technical Recruiting: tech recruiting, engineering recruiting
  it_network            IT and Network: IT support, help desk, network eng, sysadmin
  controls_industrial   Controls and Industrial: controls eng, automation eng, commissioning, PLC techs


LEVEL TAXONOMY

IC Track — applies to: swe, infra, data, tpm, pm, recruiting, it_network

  IC1   Entry / New Grad         $100k-$160k     Google L3, Meta E3, Amazon L4/SDE I, MSFT 59
  IC2   Mid-level                $140k-$220k     Google L4, Meta E4, Amazon L5/SDE II, MSFT 61
  IC3   Senior                   $180k-$300k     Google L5, Meta E5, Amazon L6/SDE III, MSFT 63-64
  IC4   Staff                    $250k-$400k+    Google L6, Meta E6, Amazon L7/Principal, MSFT 65-66
  IC5   Principal                $350k-$600k+    Google L7, Meta E7, Amazon Distinguished, MSFT 67
  IC6   Distinguished / Fellow   Rare, company-specific. L8+

Manager Track — applies to: em; also recruiting and it_network for explicit people-management postings

  M1    Engineering Manager / TLM          Front-line, 1-2 teams. Comp equivalent to IC3-IC4
  M2    Senior EM / Group EM               Manages managers or multi-team. Comp equivalent to IC4-IC5
  M3    Director of Engineering            Owns a product area or functional domain
  M4    VP Engineering and above           Rarely appears in external postings

Controls and Industrial Track — applies to: controls_industrial ONLY

  T1    PLC Technician I/II                $60k-$85k annualized. Often on Indeed/ZipRecruiter, not Greenhouse
  T2    Senior / Lead PLC Technician       Top of technician branch
  E1    Controls / Automation Engineer     Junior to mid. $80k-$110k nationally
  E2    Senior Controls / Automation Eng   Career-level. $100k-$135k nationally; Seattle/PNW ~$120k-$140k
  E3    Staff / Principal Controls Eng     Architectural scope, quasi-management
  E4    Lead / Principal Industrial Eng    Top of IC branch for this family
  CM1   Commissioning Engineer             $90k-$130k base plus significant per diem and travel premium
  CM2   Senior / Lead Commissioning Eng


SALARY NORMALIZATION RULES

- All compensation is stored and reported as annualized USD base salary only
- For hourly postings: annual = hourly_rate * 2080
- Bonus and equity language is captured as metadata only — never written into salary_min_usd, salary_max_usd, or salary_midpoint_usd
- salary_midpoint_usd is always computed: (salary_min_usd + salary_max_usd) / 2. Never user-supplied.
- salary_spread_pct is always computed: (salary_max_usd - salary_min_usd) / salary_min_usd * 100
- Any posting where salary_spread_pct exceeds 60 must be flagged multi_level_range = true. Exclude these from single-level analysis by default. Treat their midpoints conservatively when included.


DATA SOURCES BY ROLE FAMILY

Different families live in different data ecosystems. A single collection strategy does not serve all of them.

Tech roles (swe, infra, data, tpm, pm, em):
Primary surface is first-party company career pages via ATS APIs — Greenhouse, Lever, Ashby. This is where pay transparency law generates real structured salary range data.

Recruiting:
First-party career pages for tech employers. BuiltIn by city is a useful supplementary board — it carries salary data and is tech-sector focused.

IT and Network:
First-party career pages for tech employers. BuiltIn and ZipRecruiter by metro for broader market signal. Salary.com for percentile anchors on commoditized roles.

Controls and Industrial:
Fundamentally different from all other families. Most controls and automation employers do not use Greenhouse or Lever. They use Workday, iCIMS, or proprietary career pages, and they frequently post on Indeed, ZipRecruiter, and industry-specific boards. The ATS API approach covers a smaller fraction of this universe.

Required hybrid for controls_industrial: ATS-native collection for tech-adjacent industrial companies that happen to use Greenhouse or Lever, supplemented by board-level collection from Indeed and ZipRecruiter using specific saved searches per metro and role combination. Salary.com and PayScale are useful percentile reference sources for this family.

Board-level data for controls_industrial must always be stored with source_type: board. Never mix source_type: board with source_type: company_career_page when computing employer-specific trends.


COLLECTION ARCHITECTURE

Do not use Claude Computer Use or any GUI browser agent as the primary collection layer. The majority of target companies expose clean JSON APIs. Using a browser agent for an endpoint like boards-api.greenhouse.io adds agent latency, token cost, and fragility for work a single httpx call handles in milliseconds.

Technology stack:
- Python with httpx or requests for Greenhouse, Lever, and Ashby (the structured API tier)
- Playwright or Puppeteer (headless browser) for Workday and unknown legacy ATS (the fallback tier)
- Claude API calls (not Computer Use) for LLM normalization post-collection

The browser fallback tier should be a small fraction of total poll volume, not the backbone.


ATS COLLECTION ENDPOINTS

Greenhouse — no authentication required, no stated rate limits

  GET https://boards-api.greenhouse.io/v1/boards/{board_token}/jobs?content=true

  board_token is the slug at boards.greenhouse.io/{token}. Discoverable from the company careers page. Stable indefinitely.
  Response includes: job_id, title, updated_at, requisition_id, location, content (full HTML), department hierarchy, office structure with location metadata.
  Limitation: API exposes updated_at only, not original publish date. Assign first_observed_at from the first snapshot where an external_id appears in your system.

Lever — no authentication required

  GET https://api.lever.co/v0/postings/{clientname}

  Each posting includes an optional salary object: { currency, interval, min, max } plus salary range description as HTML and plain text. Always check the salary object before falling back to description parsing.

Ashby — no authentication required

  GET https://api.ashbyhq.com/posting-api/job-board/{clientname}?includeCompensation=true

  Always include ?includeCompensation=true. Without it, compensation data is not returned.
  Response includes compensationTierSummary, scrapeableCompensationSalarySummary, and structured compensationTiers objects.

Workday — Phase 2 only, do not implement in v1

  No public-facing API. Collection requires intercepting XHR calls via Playwright or Puppeteer. Consistent URL schema: {company}.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com.

Job Boards (Layer 5 — Controls/Industrial and broader market signal)

  Indeed and ZipRecruiter via saved searches scoped to metro and role combination.
  These are market-level signals for anchoring and validation, not longitudinal employer records.
  Always store with source_type: board. Never mix with source_type: company_career_page in employer-specific trend queries.


DATABASE SCHEMA

companies table:
  company_id              UUID, primary key
  canonical_name          text, not null
  canonical_domain        text
  careers_url             text
  ats_type                text — enum: greenhouse, lever, ashby, workday, icims, indeed_only, unknown
  ats_board_token         text
  metro                   text — canonical metro key or "remote-first"
  industry                text — enum: tech, fintech, healthcare, industrial, manufacturing, other
  size_band               text — enum: seed, series_a_c, growth, public, enterprise
  polling_tier            integer — 1, 2, 3, or 99 (inactive)
  last_successful_poll    timestamptz
  salary_transparency     text — enum: high, medium, low, unknown
  role_family_tags        text array — role families observed at this company

snapshots table:
  snapshot_id     UUID, primary key
  company_id      UUID, foreign key to companies
  observed_at     timestamptz, not null
  source_url      text
  raw_payload     bytea — compressed JSON blob
  job_count       integer

postings table — the core time-series record:
  posting_id              UUID, primary key
  snapshot_id             UUID, foreign key to snapshots
  company_id              UUID, foreign key to companies
  external_id             text — the ATS platform's own job ID; stable key for versioning
  title_raw               text
  title_normalized        text
  level_code              text — IC1-IC6, M1-M4, T1-T2, E1-E4, or CM1-CM2
  role_family             text — one of the nine family codes above
  location_raw            text
  metro_normalized        text
  is_remote               boolean
  salary_min_raw          text — original string from source
  salary_min_usd          integer — annualized base, normalized
  salary_max_raw          text
  salary_max_usd          integer — annualized base, normalized
  salary_midpoint_usd     integer — computed: (min + max) / 2
  salary_spread_pct       numeric — computed: (max - min) / min * 100
  multi_level_range       boolean — computed: true when salary_spread_pct > 60
  salary_source           text — enum: structured_api, parsed_description, absent
  comp_type_tag           text — enum: base_only, base_ote_mentioned, base_bonus_equity_mentioned
  transparency_quality    text — enum: exact_range, vague, absent
  description_hash        text — SHA-256 of the job description; used for change detection and LLM cache keying
  first_observed_at       timestamptz, not null
  last_observed_at        timestamptz, not null
  status                  text — enum: active, presumed_closed. Default: active
  version_number          integer, not null, default 1
  source_type             text — enum: company_career_page, board. Default: company_career_page


CRITICAL VERSIONING RULE

When a poll detects that salary_min_usd or salary_max_usd has changed for an existing (company_id, external_id) pair, INSERT a new row with version_number incremented. Do NOT update the existing row's salary fields. Never overwrite historical salary data. This versioning is what makes statements like "this company dropped Senior SWE midpoints by 10% in Q1 while widening ranges in SF" possible. Destroying old rows destroys the product.


LLM NORMALIZATION — THREE TASKS ONLY

The LLM is called post-collection for normalization only. It does not fetch data, make scheduling decisions, route requests, or participate in any control flow.

Task 1 — Title normalization
  Input: raw title string, known company-level naming conventions for this company_id, full taxonomy reference
  Output: { normalized_title, role_family, level_code } as JSON only, no prose
  Cache key: (company_id, title_raw)

Task 2 — Salary extraction from description text
  Used when structured salary fields are absent — common for Workday and legacy ATS postings
  Input: job description HTML or plain text
  Output: { salary_min_usd, salary_max_usd, salary_source: "parsed_description" } as JSON
  Cache key: description_hash — never re-run for an unchanged description

Task 3 — Metro normalization
  Input: raw location string (e.g. "Greater Seattle Area", "SF Bay", "New York, NY")
  Output: canonical metro key as a single string
  Cache aggressively — the same location strings recur constantly across all companies and all polling cycles


POLLING SCHEDULER

Priority queue ordered by next_poll_due_at. Workers are stateless.

  Tier 1       Every 24 hours
  Tier 2       Every 3-7 days
  Tier 3       Every 2-4 weeks
  Inactive     Monthly

Error handling:
  HTTP 429 or consecutive 5xx responses trigger exponential backoff
  Three consecutive failures flag the company record for human review and suspend further polling

Scale: at 10,000 companies with an average 4-day cadence, volume is approximately 2,500 polls per day, roughly 1.75 per minute. A single scheduler process with 5-10 workers handles this comfortably at v1 scale. No distributed queue infrastructure is needed.


ANALYTICAL INTEGRITY RULES

- When querying compensation trends, always filter source_type = 'company_career_page' unless explicitly doing market-wide analysis
- When querying single-level compensation, always filter multi_level_range = false or explicitly document that wide ranges are included
- When comparing across time, use first_observed_at on each version_number row to timestamp the salary range — not the snapshot's observed_at
- Never average salary across different comp_type_tag values without flagging it — base_only and base_ote_mentioned are not comparable
- Controls and Industrial comp benchmarks are national unless a metro is specified; Seattle/PNW runs roughly $15k-$20k above national median for E2


WHAT THIS DATA CAN AND CANNOT CLAIM

Can claim:
- What employers advertised they would pay for a given profile in a given market at a given point in time
- Longitudinal trends: e.g. "Bay Area Senior SWE posted midpoints compressed between 2024 and 2026"
- Transparency analytics: which employers post narrow realistic bands vs. wide multi-level ranges, and how that shifts as pay transparency laws expand
- Unified coverage of controls, industrial, and commissioning roles alongside software and tech roles under one normalization scheme

Cannot claim:
- What candidates actually signed — closed offer data is not in this system
- That posted ranges reflect final negotiated compensation
- Coverage of employers who do not post salary ranges (these are captured with transparency_quality: absent)

If candidate-reported closed offer data is ever added, the delta between posted ranges and closed offers for the same roles at the same companies over time is itself a valuable and separately publishable product. Keep the schema clean enough to support that join.
